  "app_output_paths": {
    "_comment": "remedies/app-output-paths.sh: an app's own config sometimes defaults its save/export dialog to bare $HOME, which is why files of a given extension scatter loose there instead of landing in a canonical Documents/ subfolder. This is a CONFIG EDIT to another program, not a reversible file move (contrast app_integration above) -- CLAUDE.md's acting-authority rules put preference decisions like this behind remedies/<concern>.sh: senechal writes and tests enable/verify, Zach runs enable by hand. Each apps[] entry names a KNOWN real app (config_file/section/key checked by hand before adding here, not guessed) and the canonical_dir its output should land in instead.",
    "apps": [
      {
        "name": "example-app",
        "extension": ".ext",
        "config_file": "~/.config/example-app/settings.ini",
        "section": "general",
        "key": "save_path",
        "canonical_dir": "~/Documents/ExampleAppOutput"
      }
    ]
  },
  "estate": {
    "_comment": "The devices senechal is majordomo of. One place a new device gets added; health/*.sh read this, never their own host lists. owner = who fixes problems in that device's domain; senechal owns the gaps. expect = 'always-on' (unreachable is an outage -> FAIL) or 'intermittent' (off is normal -> PASS); omit it and unreachable stays a WARN so the missing declaration is itself visible. os = 'linux' or 'windows' on reach=ssh devices -- picks the remote health probe dialect; omit it and remote checks SKIP visibly. See ESTATE.md for prose.",
    "devices": [
      {
        "name": "mandark",
        "kind": "linux-laptop",
        "addr": "localhost",
        "reach": "local",
        "owner": "senechal",
        "expect": "intermittent",
        "notes": "primary workstation; runs the scheduler, every nightly-batch project, and senechal itself. A laptop that sleeps and travels -- never treat it being dark as an outage"
      },
      {
        "name": "dexter",
        "kind": "windows-mini-pc",
        "addr": "dexter.local",
        "reach": "ssh",
        "ssh_host": "dexter",
        "os": "linux",
        "owner": "crt (console app), gardien (backup), senechal (host health)",
        "expect": "always-on",
        "notes": "also hosts a Debian VM, and the monkey VM below; senechal already watches its scripts via remote_hosts below. Mini-PC that stays up (the always-on argument behind the 2026-07-25 'move senechal to dexter' vision item). os=linux, NOT windows, and the distinction is the TRANSPORT not the machine: `ssh dexter` lands in WSL2 (verified 2026-08-05 -- uname reports 6.18.33.2-microsoft-standard-WSL2), so the health probe must be the Linux one. senechal.py's scan_remote_path separately shells out to powershell.exe FROM that WSL session when it wants the Windows side. Declared windows here until 2026-08-05, which sent a bare `powershell` the WSL shell does not have: rc=127, reported as a SKIP blaming key auth that in fact worked."
      },
      {
        "name": "monkey",
        "kind": "linux-vm",
        "addr": "monkey.tail893f2c.ts.net",
        "reach": "ssh",
        "ssh_host": "monkey",
        "os": "linux",
        "expect": "always-on",
        "owner": "realisateur (accounts, see MONKEY.md), scheduler (dispatch + rotation), senechal (host health)",
        "notes": "The self-dev host: a VirtualBox guest on dexter, reached over the tailnet by MagicDNS name (the short name does not resolve on mandark). Ubuntu 24.04.4, 118G root. One UNIX account per project (bibliothecaire, chezz, ecosim, vim-arcade), each with its own checkouts/creds/deploy keys, plus zach as the hands account. expect=always-on ON PURPOSE despite being a VM: four of those accounts carry live crontabs (verified 2026-08-05 via `ssh monkey 'sudo -n crontab -u <u> -l'`), so unreachable means dispatch is not running -- a real finding, not a laptop being closed. Registered 2026-08-05; see ESTATE.md, 'The notice channel moved'."
      },
      {
        "name": "potato",
        "kind": "raspberry-pi-3b+",
        "addr": "192.168.0.45",
        "reach": "ssh",
        "ssh_host": "potato",
        "os": "linux",
        "owner": "crt",
        "expect": "intermittent",
        "notes": "CRT console hardware, ~905MB RAM. MEMORY-CONSTRAINED: keep checks lean, no agent resident (see crt/POTATO.md). An installation that gets powered on to use, not a server"
      },
      {
        "name": "ha-pi",
        "kind": "raspberry-pi",
        "addr": "192.168.0.39",
        "reach": "ping",
        "owner": "home-assistant",
        "expect": "always-on",
        "notes": "Home Assistant OS, homeassistant.local. Config/automation delegated entirely to the home-assistant project; senechal tracks liveness only. No SSH by design. Home automation hub -- it being dark IS an outage"
      },
      {
        "name": "router",
        "kind": "network",
        "addr": "192.168.0.1",
        "reach": "ping",
        "owner": "senechal (gap -- no project owns network gear)",
        "expect": "always-on",
        "notes": "default gateway. Identified 2026-07-25: ARRIS cable gateway (admin UI banner '(c) Copyright 2011-2014, ARRIS Group, Inc.', firmware build 102414); exact model number not shown in the banner"
      }
    ],
    "_footprint_comment": "The second registry: things a project INSTALLED on a device -- systemd units, ufw holes, authorized_keys lines, config files -- that outlive the session that installed them. notify-senechal fires at install time; this is what notices when one has become a corpse. health/dead-config.sh probes each entry read-only and never removes anything. status: live / retiring (agreed dead, still installed -- nagged every run until gone) / retired (should be absent; flagged loudly if it comes back). kind: systemd-system-unit, systemd-user-unit, listening-port, path, authorized-keys. retire is the exact command that finishes the job -- write it now, while you still remember.",
    "footprint": [
      {
        "id": "example-unit",
        "host": "mandark",
        "owner": "some-project",
        "kind": "systemd-user-unit",
        "target": "example.timer",
        "status": "live",
        "retire": "systemctl --user disable --now example.timer && rm ~/.config/systemd/user/example.timer",
        "notes": "why it exists, and what would make it dead"
      },
      {
        "id": "garde-nightly-mandark",
        "host": "mandark",
        "owner": "gardien",
        "kind": "systemd-user-unit",
        "target": "garde-nightly.timer",
        "status": "live",
        "retire": "systemctl --user disable --now garde-nightly.timer && rm ~/.config/systemd/user/garde-nightly.{timer,service} && systemctl --user daemon-reload",
        "why": "THE ESTATE'S BACKUP -- and until 2026-08-03 senechal did not know it existed. It succeeded the gardien.* units (re-probed this date: those are genuinely absent, and their entry is correctly marked retired), but nothing added its replacement here, so estate-health's own backup check was still pointed at gardien.service and reported 'no backup is configured at all' while a backup ran nightly and succeeded. ExecStart is `garde media run --all-pending`, which makes this the ONLY machine consumer of any bashified verb in the ecosystem. Only the TIMER is named: dead-config.sh already pairs a timer with its .service (the 'gardien shape' its own test suite names), so listing both would be a second copy of a fact the checker derives -- and this field is matched literally, so a brace expansion here reads as one impossible unit name."
      },
      {
        "id": "synergy-tray-autostart",
        "host": "mandark",
        "owner": "zach",
        "kind": "path",
        "target": "~/.config/autostart/synergy.desktop",
        "status": "live",
        "retire": "rm ~/.config/autostart/synergy.desktop",
        "notes": "Found undeclared 2026-08-05 (hf7y/senechal#12): the tray-icon GUI (`~/.local/bin/synergy`, spawning `synergys` as its child, one listener on :24800) is the actual autostart path in use, live since 2026-07-28 -- not the headless `synergy-server.service` systemd unit this issue was originally filed to bring tray-autostart back to. That headless unit is disabled/inactive. Whether to build a `--tray` mode in setup-synergy-server.sh so this .desktop has a real uninstaller, or retire the headless unit outright now that the tray path already does the job, is an open preference decision (#12) -- this entry only closes the smaller gap, that senechal's own footprint registry didn't know this file existed."
      },
      {
        "id": "claudequota-plasmoid",
        "host": "mandark",
        "owner": "zach",
        "kind": "path",
        "target": "~/.local/share/plasma/plasmoids/com.docusketch.claudequota",
        "status": "live",
        "retire": "rm -rf ~/.local/share/plasma/plasmoids/com.docusketch.claudequota",
        "notes": "Third-party KDE plasmoid (Damian, docusketch), installed by hand, no repo. Undeclared until found while working hf7y/senechal#4 ('add burn line to weekly usage as well. display burn line as red line overlay on quota bar'). remedies/claudequota-burn-line.sh patches contents/ui/main.qml to add the missing red on-bar marker (the 5h and week windows already had the burn RATE as text; neither had a mark on the bar itself)."
      }
    ],
    "_crontab_comment": "The third registry: crontab lines another project's own account added on a host, filed via the `crontab` front door (registry/front-doors.json) so a cron change is senechal-visible instead of arriving as a hand-written prose issue (hf7y/senechal#362). Separate from estate.footprint because footprint's target is restricted to a unit name, port number or absolute path and cannot carry a schedule or a command line. status: live / retiring / retired, same lifecycle as footprint. Tag with the crontab line's own trailing `# <owner>:<mechanism>:TICK` comment so this and sync-crontab can both find it.",
    "crontab": [
      {
        "tag": "ecosim:ecosim-sensor:TICK",
        "host": "monkey",
        "account": "ecosim",
        "owner": "ecosim",
        "schedule": "7,37 * * * *",
        "command": "ECOSIM_SENSOR_BIN=/usr/local/bin/sonde /home/ecosim/Documents/Projects/ecosim/bin/ecosim-sensor-tick.sh",
        "status": "live",
        "retire": "crontab -l | grep -v 'ecosim:ecosim-sensor:TICK' | crontab -",
        "notes": "Added 2026-08-18 (hf7y/ecosim#48): the sensor suite was built and contract-tested but wired to no clock, while .gitignore asserted it ran every 30 minutes. Zach ruled run-rather-than-delete. Prior crontab backed up to /tmp/ecosim-cron.bak on monkey. ECOSIM_SENSOR_BIN is set explicitly because the script's own default is a per-account verb build and verbs went host-wide 2026-08-11 -- there is no ~/.local/share/verb-builds on the ecosim account, so the default would exit 3 forever (hf7y/ecosim#79). Writes ~ecosim/.local/share/ecosim-sensor/{run.log,latest.txt,archive.jsonl}."
      }
    ],
    "_verbs_comment": "The fourth registry: verbs `installe` put on PATH, filed via the `verb` front door (registry/front-doors.json) so an install is senechal-visible instead of failing UNDECLARED (hf7y/senechal#414 -- the door did not exist, so no `installe verb` call had ever landed one). Separate from estate.footprint because footprint's target is restricted to a unit name, port number or absolute path and cannot carry which project owns the verb or which build it is pinned to. status: live / retiring / retired, same lifecycle as footprint and crontab. No prober reads this yet -- #414 scoped the fix to the filing landing at all, not to a dead-verb check.",
    "verbs": [
      {
        "name": "example-verb",
        "host": "mandark",
        "account": "zach",
        "project": "some-project",
        "build": "2026-08-24T033024Z",
        "status": "live",
        "retire": "installe retire example-verb"
      }
    ],
    "_secrets_comment": "The credential inventory: WHERE a credential lives, WHAT it is for, and HOW TO MINT A NEW ONE. Never the value -- this is a registry, not a keychain (Zach, 2026-08-13: a re-mintable credential is better held once and registered than copied into a vault that then needs its own escrow). health/secret-registry.sh probes presence and mode and never reads contents. mode is a CEILING: wider than declared is a finding. recovery: remint (re-issue it -- prefer this, and keep ONE copy; on this estate everything is remint) / escrow (vocabulary for a credential that genuinely cannot be re-issued -- none currently qualify). An entry whose host is not this one is PROBED over BatchMode ssh using estate.devices' ssh_host; only a host with no ssh_host, or one that does not answer, is a SKIP. offhost: forbid (no plaintext copy may leave this host -- checked against gardien's own backup set list) / allow (a copy off-host is deliberate; say why in notes). status: live / retired (destroyed on purpose -- gone is the pass, a reappearance is the finding). mint is the whole point: a credential with no runbook is unrecoverable no matter how many copies exist.",
    "secrets": [
      {
        "id": "example-token",
        "host": "mandark",
        "owner": "some-project",
        "path": "~/.config/some-project/api_token",
        "purpose": "what this credential authenticates, and for whom",
        "mode": "600",
        "recovery": "remint",
        "verify": "a command that proves this credential still WORKS -- presence is the weak question, a revoked token is still a file. Run with --verify; never part of the cron-safe hourly check, because these touch the network.",
        "reprovision": "the ONE reissue field: the command or runbook that issues a new one, and revokes the old. --reprovision prints it and executes nothing, because minting supersedes a live credential and that stays a human act. An entry without this is unrecoverable no matter how many copies exist, so the check WARNs.",
        "reprovision_cost": "low | medium | high | none | unknown -- the honest half. A recorded runbook is not a cheap one: ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 is one ssh-keygen away and still high, because nothing records which hosts trust it. high and unknown are surfaced, so \"straightforward to reissue\" is tested rather than assumed.",
        "offhost": "forbid",
        "status": "live",
        "notes": "NEVER put the value here. tools/export-registry.py runs senechal.py's looks_secret() over every exported value and refuses to commit the registry if one matches."
      }
    ],
    "_taste_comment": "A 'taste' is a Zach shell/config preference applied identically on every home he has a shell on. lib/taste-block.sh installs/verifies a marker-delimited block in a file; remedies/<id>.sh reads this list's `hosts` for its own id and applies/checks the block there (local directly, or over ssh via estate.devices' ssh_host). Onboarding a new home is adding its device name to a taste's `hosts` array here -- no script change. file is ~-relative, same value on every host. status: enabled / disabled (should be ABSENT everywhere). See CONCERNS.md.",
    "taste": [
      {
        "id": "colorhash-prompt",
        "description": "PS1 colors \\u and \\h with two INDEPENDENT hashes (not one hash of the combined string), so a familiar user on an unfamiliar host (or vice versa) is visible at a glance without reading it; @ stays uncolored. Computed live at shell start, so one identical block works unmodified on every host.",
        "file": ".bashrc",
        "hosts": [
          "mandark",
          "dexter",
          "monkey"
        ],
        "status": "enabled",
        "owner": "senechal",
        "notes": "Filed via `scheduler -i` 2026-07-29 22:44 (.scheduler/FOCUS.md): 'bashrc that makes user and host colors hashes of ids ... install on all systems'. Content and remedy: remedies/colorhash-prompt.sh (config edit to Zach's shell -- preference decision, so it ships as enable/verify per CLAUDE.md's acting-authority rules, not applied unattended). Still declares `hosts`, deliberately unmigrated: cfg_taste reads it as shorthand for zach@<host>, which is what it always meant."
      },
      {
        "id": "claude-slash-commands",
        "description": "Zach's interactive Claude slash commands (/bashify, /cloture, /ideate) present and CURRENT in every home he might type them in. Content is generated by realisateur's bin/install-shims.sh from its own .claude/commands/*.md (every file declaring `scope: user`); senechal does not hold a copy and never will -- it asserts the estate-wide property realisateur's own --check cannot see, because that check only ever inspects the host it runs on.",
        "file": ".claude/commands/",
        "homes": [
          {
            "host": "mandark",
            "account": "zach"
          },
          {
            "host": "dexter",
            "account": "zach"
          },
          {
            "host": "monkey",
            "account": "zach"
          }
        ],
        "status": "enabled",
        "owner": "realisateur (generates and owns the command content) / senechal (owns knowing WHICH homes need them and whether they are current there)",
        "notes": "Zach 2026-08-06: these belong in every home where he might interactively call an agent -- every zach@ account, and anywhere the hf7y verb build lands -- and they must be tracked by something OTHER than `installe`. installe owns PATH symlinks out of a verb build (~/.local/bin/<verb> -> verb-builds/current/..., retired via `installe retire <verb>`); slash commands are a different artifact class with a different lifecycle: per-home files under ~/.claude/commands/, needed in homes that may have no verb build at all. Routing them through installe would mean inventing a fake verb and coupling 'Zach can type /ideate here' to 'this account has a build'. Hence this registry. `homes` (not `hosts`) because monkey carries one UNIX account per self-dev project plus zach as the hands account, and only zach@monkey wants these -- the other nine run agents non-interactively. Remedy: remedies/claude-slash-commands.sh; verify is wired into health/estate-health.sh. State when filed 2026-08-06: drifted on mandark, stale hand-copy on dexter, entirely absent in zach@monkey."
      }
    ]
  },
  "self_dev": {
    "_comment": "The self-dev teardown inventory: what must be GONE from mandark, and what must SURVIVE, for 'mandark is no longer a self-dev host' to be true. Read by health/no-self-dev.sh, which is read-only and removes nothing. Zach 2026-07-30: mandark becomes a regular user machine plus a jump box; gardien owns the backup details, realisateur owns where self-dev goes next. THE MUST-REMAIN HALF IS NOT DECORATION -- it is the guard that stops a teardown from eating products that merely happen to be agent-built (gardien's backups, bibliothecaire's OCR intake, senechal's own health timer). verdict: must-be-absent (present => FAIL, with the retire command) / must-remain (absent => FAIL, teardown overshot) / deferred (cannot be decided until the destination host is named => SKIP, never a pass). kind: crontab-active (target is an ERE matched against uncommented crontab lines) / path (one file, dir or symlink; ~ expanded) / glob (target is a glob; absent means zero matches -- used where enumerating 22 near-identical loop scripts would be noise) / systemd-user-unit / systemd-system-unit (unit FILE present, not merely active -- an installed-but-stopped unit is still footprint) / claude-hook (target is a substring searched in ~/.claude/settings.json) / deferred-note. phase orders the work for the reaped migration brief (ecosystem1-vault, senechal/MIGRATION-OFF-MANDARK.md) and is reported, not enforced: the test asks one question, 'is self-dev gone', and every straggler is a FAIL regardless of which phase it belongs to.",
    "host": "mandark",
    "_destination_comment": "destination_host is the host self-dev MOVES TO. Named by Zach 2026-08-06: monkey. Until this date it was unnamed, and two phase-5 items reported SKIP for exactly that reason -- 'not decidable until the destination host is named' was an honest answer, not a stub. Naming it is what makes them decidable, so this field is load-bearing and not documentation. Re-probed 2026-08-06 via `ssh monkey`: up 2d, Ubuntu 24.04, 105G free on /, ELEVEN accounts (zach + ten self-dev: ecosim, vim-arcade, bibliothecaire, chezz armed; gardien, baudin, crt, groc-mangr, nine-speakers, sequestria provisioned and NOT armed -- senechal#69). Its checkouts clone from GitHub, NOT from mandark's ~/git-remotes -- verified by reading /home/ecosim/Documents/Projects/ecosim/.git/config, whose origin is git@github.com:hf7y/ecosim.git. That single fact is what settles bare-remotes below.",
    "destination_host": "monkey",
    "items": [
      {
        "id": "crontab-dispatch",
        "kind": "crontab-active",
        "target": "(usage-paced-runner|scheduler|weight-audit|sweep|nightly-batch|bug-sweep)",
        "owner": "scheduler",
        "phase": 1,
        "verdict": "must-be-absent",
        "why": "The dispatcher. Emptied 2026-07-29 by realisateur for THE PLAY (run 3), with the whole crontab backed up to realisateur/archive/crontab-backups/mandark-crontab-2026-07-29T1425.bak. So this entry does not ask for work -- it asserts that a done thing STAYS done. It is the one item most likely to silently regress, because scheduler's own bin/sync-crontab.sh exists to reinstall exactly these lines and the premise of THE PLAY was that scheduler brings dispatch back up itself. If this ever probes present again, dispatch was regenerated onto a host that is supposed to be out of the rotation.",
        "retire": "crontab -l | grep -v -E '(usage-paced-runner|scheduler|weight-audit|sweep)' | crontab -   # and unregister mandark in scheduler's schedule/_runner.conf, or it comes back"
      },
      {
        "id": "scheduler-path-entries",
        "kind": "glob",
        "target": "~/.local/bin/{scheduler,scheduler-dev-cycle.sh,usage-gate.sh,usage-paced-runner.sh,usage-paced-runner-dexter.sh}",
        "owner": "scheduler",
        "phase": 1,
        "verdict": "must-be-absent",
        "why": "Symlinks into the scheduler repo -- live entry points on PATH. Worse than dangling: they still WORK. Anything (a cron line, a hook, a human, an agent reading a stale doc) that types `scheduler` gets a functioning dispatcher back. CORRECTED 2026-08-06: this said they point into '~/Documents/Project Archive/scheduler' and that 'archiving the repo was necessary and not sufficient'. Re-probed -- the repo is NOT archived. There is no scheduler under ~/Documents/Project Archive at all; `readlink -f ~/.local/bin/scheduler-dev-cycle.sh` resolves to /home/zach/Documents/Projects/scheduler/bin/scheduler-dev-cycle.sh, and that checkout is LIVE -- 27 schedule/*.conf files and a commit written 2026-08-05 23:25. So this item is worse than it read, not better: these are not stale pointers at parked code, they are current pointers at a repo something is actively committing to. The retire command is unchanged; the urgency is not.",
        "retire": "rm ~/.local/bin/{scheduler,scheduler-dev-cycle.sh,usage-gate.sh,usage-paced-runner.sh}"
      },
      {
        "id": "scheduler-binary-backup",
        "kind": "glob",
        "target": "~/.local/bin/scheduler.bak.*",
        "owner": "scheduler",
        "phase": 1,
        "verdict": "must-be-absent",
        "why": "A 118KB real copy of the dispatcher (not a symlink) left on PATH as scheduler.bak.2026-07-28. It survives archiving the repo AND deleting the symlinks above, which makes it the last thing anyone would check and the only one that needs no other file to run.",
        "retire": "rm ~/.local/bin/scheduler.bak.*"
      },
      {
        "id": "batch-loop-wrappers",
        "kind": "glob",
        "target": "~/.local/bin/*-nightly-batch-loop.sh ~/.local/bin/*-bug-sweep-loop.sh ~/.local/bin/*-batch-loop.sh ~/.local/bin/*-batch-loop.sh.pre-* ~/.local/bin/chezz-bug-sweep-precheck.sh ~/.local/bin/scheduler-overnight-dev.sh",
        "owner": "realisateur (the wrapper convention); each project (its own wrapper)",
        "phase": 2,
        "verdict": "must-be-absent",
        "why": "The per-project unattended-run wrappers -- ~22 files, one per participating project, each the thing cron actually invoked. Matched by glob rather than enumerated because a list of 22 near-identical names in this file would rot the first time a project is added or renamed, and the glob names what it retires just as precisely. These have no generator (same class as ESTATE.md finding 5), so deleting them is irreversible in practice: SNAPSHOT THE DIRECTORY FIRST. They are also the honest inventory of who was in the rotation -- read the list before deleting it, because after this step nothing on mandark records that abletim, aedile, chezz, ecosim, groc-mangr, nine-speakers, quatre-vingt-douze, secretaire, sequestria, vim-arcade, vkv-inventory or wtul ever ran here.",
        "retire": "tar czf ~/self-dev-teardown-snapshots/local-bin-$(date +%Y%m%dT%H%M).tar.gz -C ~/.local bin && rm ~/.local/bin/*-batch-loop.sh ~/.local/bin/*-nightly-batch-loop.sh ~/.local/bin/*-bug-sweep-loop.sh ~/.local/bin/chezz-bug-sweep-precheck.sh ~/.local/bin/scheduler-overnight-dev.sh"
      },
      {
        "id": "ecosim-sensor-tick",
        "kind": "path",
        "target": "~/.local/bin/ecosim-sensor-tick",
        "owner": "realisateur",
        "phase": 2,
        "verdict": "must-be-absent",
        "why": "Symlink to realisateur/bin/ecosim-sensor-tick.sh -- the periodic sampler that fed ecosim, i.e. instrumentation OF the self-dev rotation. Distinct from ~/.local/bin/ecosim-sensor (the ecosim project's own binary), which is a product and is listed must-remain below. Deleting the wrong one of the two is the easy mistake here, which is why both are named.",
        "retire": "rm ~/.local/bin/ecosim-sensor-tick"
      },
      {
        "id": "run-markers",
        "kind": "glob",
        "target": "~/.local/share/*-nightly-batch ~/.local/share/*-bug-sweep",
        "owner": "realisateur (convention); each project (its own marker)",
        "phase": 3,
        "verdict": "must-be-absent",
        "why": "18 marker directories recording last-run state per project, the mechanism that made runs idempotent. Harmless if left, which is exactly why they get left -- and then a future run on ANOTHER host reads a marker that says work already happened here and skips it. Cheap to delete, and their absence is the clean witness that nothing on mandark is keeping rotation state any more.",
        "retire": "rm -rf ~/.local/share/*-nightly-batch ~/.local/share/*-bug-sweep"
      },
      {
        "id": "session-marker-hook",
        "kind": "claude-hook",
        "target": "realisateur/bin/session-marker.sh",
        "owner": "realisateur",
        "phase": 4,
        "verdict": "must-be-absent",
        "why": "SessionStart/SessionEnd hooks in ~/.claude/settings.json calling realisateur's session-marker.sh acquire/release -- the concurrency lock that let paced dispatch know an interactive session was live. THIS ONE FIRES ON EVERY CLAUDE CODE SESSION, including the ordinary interactive use that mandark is being kept for. Once realisateur's repo moves or is archived the hook fails on every session start; a hook whose command is missing is exactly the silent-guard failure CLAUDE.md's 'a missing command is a finding' rule exists for. Remove the hooks BEFORE moving the repo, not after.",
        "retire": "edit ~/.claude/settings.json and delete the SessionStart and SessionEnd hook blocks (backup already at ~/.claude/settings.json.bak) -- or use the update-config skill"
      },
      {
        "id": "ecosystem-protocol-shims",
        "kind": "glob",
        "target": "~/.local/bin/{notify-senechal,check-project-busy,focus-commit,closeout-lint,hygiene-lint,milestone-audit,steward-survey,ecosystem-survey,precipitation-scan,silence-audit}",
        "owner": "realisateur",
        "phase": 4,
        "verdict": "must-be-absent",
        "why": "The cross-project protocol commands -- ten of them, of which three (notify-senechal, check-project-busy, focus-commit) are the guards CLAUDE.md mandates for every write that reaches outside a repo. NO LONGER BLOCKED, 2026-08-06. This entry said 'THIS IS THE ITEM THAT NEEDS A DECISION, NOT A DELETION' on the strength of ESTATE.md finding 5: the shims were hand-written, untracked and had NO generator, so deleting them was unrecoverable. Re-probed and that is no longer true -- realisateur/bin/install-shims.sh exists (dated 2026-08-04) with a --check mode, and `bash install-shims.sh --check` reports ok for all ten. Finding 5's own close check is met and it is closed. Deleting these is now fully recoverable by one command, on any host with a realisateur checkout -- and monkey already has one inside every self-dev account (verified: /home/ecosim/Documents/Projects/realisateur/bin/install-shims.sh). WHAT ACTUALLY REMAINS is smaller and specific, and it is not about recoverability: every shim execs a script INSIDE the realisateur working checkout (/home/zach/Documents/Projects/realisateur/bin/<name>.sh), which is incident 3 of 2026-08-06 verbatim -- closeout-lint ran an unmerged feature branch for every project on this machine because the checkout happened to be on one. So on mandark these are not merely self-dev residue, they are live exec-out-of-a-checkout couplings. senechal still wants notify-senechal reachable from mandark ('senechal owns knowing what exists on the host' does not stop being true when the rotation leaves -- a jump box other people reach into is exactly where undeclared config accumulates), but wanting the COMMAND is not the same as wanting it wired to a working checkout. That is the residual question for Zach, and it is a preference about mandark's jump-box role, not a blocker on the teardown.",
        "retire": "Recoverable, no longer blocked. On monkey (or any host keeping them): `bash ~/Documents/Projects/realisateur/bin/install-shims.sh` regenerates all ten; `--check` verifies without writing. On mandark, retiring them is `rm ~/.local/bin/{notify-senechal,check-project-busy,focus-commit,closeout-lint,hygiene-lint,milestone-audit,steward-survey,ecosystem-survey,precipitation-scan,silence-audit}` -- but ASK ZACH FIRST about notify-senechal specifically, which senechal wants to keep reachable from mandark and which today execs the realisateur working checkout. Note install-shims.sh also installs three slash commands (/bashify, /cloture, /ideate) and reports all three drifted on mandark as of 2026-08-06 -- realisateur's, filed with the shim work."
      },
      {
        "id": "front-door-watch",
        "kind": "systemd-user-unit",
        "target": "front-door-watch.service",
        "owner": "front-door",
        "phase": 4,
        "verdict": "must-be-absent",
        "why": "Polls github hf7y/front-door every 120s and relays knocks, amendment PRs, failed CI and milestone deadlines to WhatsApp. Installed 2026-07-29 (Restart=always). Listed as must-be-absent for a specific reason worth stating rather than assuming: its stated justification is that GitHub Actions cannot reach the WhatsApp bridge because the bridge is 127.0.0.1-only, so mandark HAS to be the bridge. That premise is about self-dev CI, and it dissolves when self-dev moves to a host that can host the bridge itself. If Zach wants WhatsApp relay from a jump box regardless, flip this to must-remain -- but it should be a choice, not residue, because Restart=always means it will keep polling forever whether or not anyone is still developing.",
        "retire": "systemctl --user disable --now front-door-watch.service && rm ~/.config/systemd/user/front-door-watch.service && systemctl --user daemon-reload   # see the 2026-07-29 20:48 FOCUS.md entry for front-door's own full teardown, including the hermes .env revert"
      },
      {
        "id": "project-checkouts",
        "kind": "glob",
        "target": "~/Documents/Projects/{bibliothecaire,chezz,dcp-gate-site,dog,french-textbook,maitre,realisateur,scheduler,space-canon,wtul,zach.audio}",
        "owner": "realisateur",
        "phase": 5,
        "verdict": "must-be-absent",
        "why": "Inert once phases 1-4 land -- a checkout with no dispatcher, no wrapper and no marker does nothing on its own, so this is deliberately NOT gated on. Whether the clones stay for hand editing on a jump box is Zach's call. THE BLOCKER MOVED, 2026-08-06: it is no longer 'no destination host exists' -- monkey exists, is provisioned with ten self-dev accounts and clones from GitHub, so nothing here is load-bearing for self-dev any more. What is left is a preference senechal must not invent, so this stays SKIP and can never read as a pass. Naming the new blocker matters: a SKIP whose stated reason is stale is indistinguishable from a check nobody has looked at. NOTE the sharper question tonight's four incidents raise (the reaped migration brief (ecosystem1-vault, senechal/MIGRATION-OFF-MANDARK.md), 'Why this became urgent'): the danger is not that clones EXIST on mandark, it is that things EXEC out of them -- senechal-health.service, ~/.local/bin/closeout-lint and ecosim-sensor-tick all did, which is how `git checkout` silently repointed production three times in one night. Keeping the clones is defensible; keeping a unit whose ExecStart lives inside one is not. senechal's own checkout is separately must-remain below. DECIDED 2026-08-11, Zach-directed: mandark stops being a jump box for hand-editing clones; no more clones under ~/Documents/Projects except senechal's own (separately must-remain below).",
        "retire": "Removal goes through `fauche` (a gardien tool, already on PATH), which checks each repo is fully recoverable (pushed, clean, no live PATH consumer, prose vaulted) before it is eligible, and emits ~/Documents/Projects/REMOVE-RECOVERABLE-REPOS.sh for Zach to run himself -- senechal deletes nothing. RE-PROBED 2026-08-15 and the earlier reading was an artefact: this entry said \"as of 2026-08-11, fauche list reports every repo KEEP status\", which was true only because /srv/ecosystem1-vault does not exist on mandark, so fauche answered \"whether the prose is consigned is unknown\" for EVERY repo and KEEP was the honest floor. Given a real vault (tools/vault.sh run fauche list, hf7y/senechal#313) that blocker drops to zero and two repos are REMOVABLE: baudin and crt. .claude and dog are BLIND. The rest are KEEP on real per-repo blockers -- unpushed branches, live PATH consumers (bashify, ecosim-sensor-tick, canon, wtul-rip-dev). The migration brief is reaped: ecosystem1-vault, senechal/MIGRATION-OFF-MANDARK.md."
      },
      {
        "id": "bare-remotes",
        "kind": "glob",
        "target": "~/git-remotes/*.git",
        "owner": "scheduler / realisateur",
        "phase": 5,
        "verdict": "must-be-absent",
        "why": "DECIDED 2026-08-06, and the decision reversed the premise. This was deferred because ~/git-remotes was believed to be where scheduler jobs CLONE FROM, making it infrastructure the next host would need. Re-probed once the destination was named and BOTH halves of that premise are false. (1) monkey does not use it: /home/ecosim/Documents/Projects/ecosim/.git/config has origin git@github.com:hf7y/ecosim.git, and every self-dev account is provisioned with per-repo GitHub deploy keys (senechal#69) -- the destination host clones from GitHub, so mandark's bare mirrors are legacy, not infrastructure. (2) it holds no unique history: the standing rule here was 'do not delete on the strength of GitHub has it without checking per repo', so it was checked per repo -- for all 15 bare repos, EVERY ref tip resolves as an existing commit via `gh api repos/hf7y/<n>/commits/<sha>`, INCLUDING basheur.git, the one this file specifically worried about. That flips the verdict from deferred to must-be-absent: a legacy mirror of fully-published history, on a host that is becoming a jump box. Still only a printed retire command -- senechal deletes nothing -- and the mirror check is named in it so nobody re-runs the rm on a stale assumption.",
        "retire": "RE-VERIFY FIRST, then delete. For each ~/git-remotes/*.git, confirm every `git --git-dir=<d> show-ref` sha still resolves via `gh api repos/hf7y/<name>/commits/<sha>` (all 15 did on 2026-08-06); then `tar czf ~/self-dev-teardown-snapshots/git-remotes-$(date +%Y%m%dT%H%M).tar.gz -C ~ git-remotes && rm -rf ~/git-remotes`. Note garde.json already backs up ~/git-remotes to dexter-d, so the snapshot is a second belt, not the only one."
      },
      {
        "id": "gardien-timers",
        "kind": "glob",
        "target": "~/.config/systemd/user/{garde-nightly.timer,garde-nightly.service}",
        "owner": "gardien",
        "phase": 0,
        "verdict": "must-remain",
        "why": "Backups. A jump box needs backing up at least as much as a dev host did, and Zach has assigned the backup details to gardien for this migration. Guarded here because these are systemd --user units installed by an agent-run project, sitting in the same directory as the units being torn down -- a phase-4 sweep of ~/.config/systemd/user is the exact plausible mistake. A TIMER KEEPER IMPLIES ITS SERVICE: the paired .service is named explicitly because health/project-unwired.sh exempts by unit name, and listing only the timer left the service reading as self-dev wiring to be deleted -- which would leave a timer firing into a missing unit. RETARGETED 2026-08-06, and the correction is the interesting part. This entry named gardien.timer / gardien-check-stale.timer / gardien-git-hygiene.timer and their services, which are genuinely absent -- so it read 'GONE, and it was supposed to stay' and stood as the one WRONGLY REMOVED item in the report, i.e. senechal's own two-sided guard accusing a teardown of eating the estate's backups. It had not. Those three units were deliberately retired 2026-08-01 and REPLACED by garde-nightly.{timer,service} (the comment header inside garde-nightly.service says so in its own words), and senechal already knew: ESTATE.md finding 1 is CLOSED on exactly this, and estate.footprint's garde-nightly-mandark entry has recorded it since 2026-08-03. Re-probed 2026-08-06: garde-nightly.timer is enabled + active, last run 2026-08-05 03:39:37 exit 0, 'all 4 set(s) copied and proven'. THE REAL FINDING IS THE DRIFT: senechal carries two registries naming the same units -- estate.footprint and self_dev.items -- and updating one did not update the other, so a five-day-stale copy manufactured a false backups-are-gone alarm for five days. Same class as the senechal.json / senechal.json.example drift the reaped migration brief (ecosystem1-vault, senechal/MIGRATION-OFF-MANDARK.md) already complains about, one level in. A must-remain entry that names a retired unit does not fail safe: it cries wolf, and the next reader learns to discount the loudest guard in the file.",
        "retire": "n/a -- must remain"
      },
      {
        "id": "senechal-health-timer",
        "kind": "glob",
        "target": "~/.config/systemd/user/senechal-health.{service,timer}",
        "owner": "senechal",
        "phase": 0,
        "verdict": "must-remain",
        "why": "senechal's hourly estate health check, enabled by hand 2026-07-28. senechal is estate management, not self-dev: knowing what mandark's config is and whether its disk is filling stays valuable when mandark is a jump box, and the alert path (KDE Connect + notify-send) needs Zach's live session bus, so it cannot be relocated to a headless host without losing the channel. senechal's nightly-batch wrapper leaves with the rotation; senechal's watching stays. ASSUMPTION, made explicit because Zach did not answer it: if senechal should also leave, this entry flips and remedies/estate-health-timer.sh disable is the undo.",
        "retire": "n/a -- must remain"
      },
      {
