        "phase": 0,
        "verdict": "must-be-absent",
        "why": "Six root units (intake, intake-ocr, intake-health) forming a document intake and OCR pipeline. Was must-remain (a user-facing product that happened to be agent-built, protected from self-dev-teardown collateral damage) until Zach directed 2026-08-04 that the whole pipeline is to be deleted outright -- a decommission decision, not a self-dev distinction, so must-be-absent is now the accurate read even though nothing has actually come down yet.",
        "retire": "owner is bibliothecaire, not senechal -- filed via scheduler -i bibliothecaire 2026-08-04 (commit 1e4c8ef in that repo's FOCUS.md). Their own mechanism: systemd/install.sh --uninstall (6 root units) + smb/install-intake-share.sh --uninstall (bibintake + bibquotes shares) + remove the bibscan service account. DEPENDENCY: bibquotes is consumed by potato (crt's Pi) -- flagged to bibliothecaire to coordinate with crt before the share actually disappears.",
        "notes": "Filed the same day, now moot once this is torn down: bibliothecaire-intake-health.service's FAIL was a false alarm (root-vs-zach $HOME mismatch in intake.py:1200's garde-ledger lookup, not a real backup gap) -- see commit 392be18 in bibliothecaire's FOCUS.md for the diagnosis, kept here for the record even though the fix is superseded by the deletion. Data-retention question RESOLVED 2026-08-04: not deleted -- the tracked intake corpus (/home/zach/bibliothecaire-intake, 206 items) moves to monkey on dexter, triggered for 2026-08-11 (filed 92bedb7 in bibliothecaire's FOCUS.md; bibliothecaire@monkey is already provisioned). Also filed same commit, no date: bibquotes is to pivot from samba to HTTP for both serving and intake, superseding the samba-share dependency line above once built."
      },
      {
        "id": "hermes-gateway",
        "kind": "systemd-user-unit",
        "target": "hermes-gateway.service",
        "owner": "hermes",
        "phase": 0,
        "verdict": "must-remain",
        "why": "The WhatsApp/Baileys bridge on 127.0.0.1:3000. hermes' own product, not self-dev tooling, even though front-door started it and edited ~/.hermes/.env to make it work (see the 2026-07-29 20:48 FOCUS.md entry -- that config edit is flagged there as most needing a second opinion, and reverting it is part of front-door's teardown, not this one). Listed must-remain so retiring front-door-watch does not take the bridge with it.",
        "retire": "n/a -- must remain",
        "notes": "TAKEN DOWN 2026-08-04, Zach-directed: systemctl --user disable --now hermes-gateway.service, then reset-failed to clear the enabled-but-FAILED-since-2026-08-02 state (root cause: the WhatsApp bridge on :3000 was not running, plus repeated model safety refusals in hermes-agent's own code -- see journalctl --user -u hermes-gateway.service around 2026-08-02 10:38). Verified inactive (dead), disabled, nothing listening on :3000. This is a reversible operational stop, not a removal: the unit file is untouched, so this row correctly still reads must-remain (still installed) and health/no-self-dev.sh should not flag it. Re-enable with systemctl --user enable --now hermes-gateway.service. SAME DAY, Zach reports hermes has moved to dexter -- 'zaxon' inside `wsl -d hermes`. NOT independently verified from mandark (dexter's estate entry currently describes 'Windows mini-PC + a Debian VM', no WSL distro named 'hermes' registered anywhere senechal tracks). Deliberately NOT flipping this row to must-be-absent on an unverified secondhand report -- see ESTATE.md open finding 12 (cross-host audit, filed 2026-08-04) for the real close condition: confirm the WSL instance is actually live and taking over the bridge role before mandark's copy is called dead weight."
      },
      {
        "id": "user-utilities",
        "kind": "glob",
        "target": "~/.local/bin/{yt-dlp,catprint,printlist,phomemo_printer,ddcast,lid-inhibit-daemon,phone-mic-setup.sh,zod,synergy}",
        "owner": "zach",
        "phase": 0,
        "verdict": "must-remain",
        "why": "Ordinary personal utilities that share ~/.local/bin with ~30 self-dev wrappers. This entry is a canary, not an inventory: it exists so that a too-broad glob during phase 2 (`rm ~/.local/bin/*.sh` would take phone-mic-setup.sh and remove-watermark.sh with it) fails this test loudly instead of being noticed months later. These are what mandark is being kept FOR.",
        "retire": "n/a -- must remain"
      },
      {
        "id": "senechal-checkout",
        "kind": "path",
        "target": "~/Documents/Projects/senechal",
        "owner": "senechal",
        "phase": 0,
        "verdict": "must-remain",
        "why": "senechal's own working copy. The health timer above runs health/estate-health.sh out of this directory, so removing it with the other checkouts in phase 5 would silently break the timer that is supposed to remain -- the unit would keep firing and its ExecStart would keep failing. Named so phase 5 cannot take it by accident.",
        "retire": "n/a -- must remain"
      }
    ]
  },
  "health": {
    "_comment": "Thresholds for health/estate-health.sh. Raise a warn threshold rather than ignoring a recurring warning -- that keeps the signal honest.",
    "disk_warn_pct": 85,
    "disk_fail_pct": 95,
    "battery_wear_warn_pct": 75,
    "temp_warn_c": 85,
    "backup_unit": "garde-nightly.service",
    "backup_max_age_hours": 36,
    "pending_updates_warn": 40,
    "smart_dump_file": "/var/lib/senechal/smart-health.txt",
    "smart_dump_max_age_hours": 48,
    "remote_ssh_timeout": 6,
    "remote_ssh_identity": "~/.ssh/senechal-estate-ed25519",
    "_comment_notify_audit": "notify_audit_warn_count: remedies/notify-audit.sh logs EVERY desktop notification on the session bus, any sender, to notify-audit.log; health/estate-health.sh's check_notify_audit summarizes it by sender on the existing hourly run and WARNs on any sender at or above this count since the last check, then truncates the raw log so it cannot bloat between runs. Default 20 -- raise it if a legitimately chatty app (a long download manager, a build tool) keeps tripping it for normal behaviour.",
    "notify_audit_warn_count": 20,
    "_comment_dead_config": "dead_config_stale_days: how long a unit may sit installed-but-inactive before health/dead-config.sh calls it suspected-dead rather than merely idle. A oneshot between runs and an abandoned service look identical in a single is-active, so the age is the whole distinction. Raise it if a legitimately infrequent job keeps getting flagged; do not silence the entry instead.",
    "dead_config_stale_days": 3,
    "_comment_notice_stale": "notice_stale_days: how long a `scheduler -i senechal`/`notify-senechal` notice (a GitHub issue on this repo, label:idea, open) may sit unabsorbed before health/unabsorbed-notices.sh nags about it. Found 2026-08-05 (hf7y/senechal#29): four had accumulated, one naming monkey -- a host with four live project accounts ESTATE.md had never heard of -- while estate-health.sh passed the whole time. The notifier's motive ends at `gh issue create`; this is the other half, same shape as dead_config_stale_days but pointed at the issue queue. Closing the issue IS the acknowledgement -- there is no separate ledger to keep honest here.",
    "notice_stale_days": 2,
    "_comment_alerts": "alert_min_severity: which health runs count as non-clean at all (fed to the routing below, and to the one remaining desktop page -- see alert_expire_minutes). 'warn' (default) = FAIL or WARN; an INCOMPLETE-only run (stale SMART dump, remote probe key not yet trusted -- known gaps in ESTATE.md) stays silent so the channel keeps meaning something. 'fail' = only real failures. 'incomplete' = any non-PASS run, the loudest setting. check_interval is what remedies/estate-health-timer.sh arms the systemd --user timer with (systemd time span syntax).",
    "alert_min_severity": "warn",
    "check_interval": "1h",
    "_comment_routing": "2026-08-11: notify-send stopped being the destination for ordinary findings -- Zach's own report was that the channel had gone dead from spam ('not actionable, creates multiple tiles, stale and sticky'), and a triage of what was actually paging showed roughly half already carry an Owner: tag naming another project and belong filed there (scheduler -i <owner>, CLAUDE.md's own delegation rule), and most of the rest are senechal's own and need no human at all -- they fold into one running GitHub issue an agent picks up on its own schedule instead (see sync_senechal_issue in lib/common.sh). A NEW finding still routes on first sight (see the flap guard in alert_if_changed for why a one-run blip does not re-route); notify_alert is left for exactly the estate going fully clean. alert_expire_minutes (default 60) is how long that one remaining desktop tile stays visible before auto-dismissing -- 0 is sticky forever. Every page also REPLACES the previous tile in place (notify-send --replace-id) rather than stacking a new one.",
    "alert_expire_minutes": 60,
    "_comment_deploy_ref": "deploy_ref: the git ref the CHECKOUT SERVING THE TIMER must contain, checked by estate-health.sh's check_deploy. senechal-health.service's ExecStart points at a live working checkout, which is also where branches get hopped -- so a merged fix can sit on origin/main and simply never run. That happened on 2026-08-05: PR #85 (alert on changes, not on levels) merged, local main stayed two commits behind, and the pre-#85 script kept paging on every hourly run -- 24 critical notifications in the 24 hours after the fix for exactly that had landed. The test is ancestry, not equality: a feature branch rebased on the mainline is running the merged code and passes; a main left behind after a merge does not. Compared against the locally known ref only, because health checks are read-only and `git fetch` writes -- an unknown ref reports SKIP, never PASS.",
    "deploy_ref": "origin/main",
    "_comment_transient_units": "Globs for failed units that are transient per-window app scopes rather than services -- health/estate-health.sh notes them by name but does not count them as failures. KDE and snapd launch each app window in a .scope with a fresh random id; a Konsole whose shell exited nonzero, or a killed Chromium, leaves that scope parked in `failed` until someone runs reset-failed. Counting them reported a crashed browser tab as estate breakage AND, because the id is new every time, made two occurrences impossible to dedupe -- so the failure count churned and an unchanged estate looked like news. Only .scope units are ever eligible, enforced in code and not by these globs, so no real .service can hide here. tools/reap-failed-scopes.sh is the acting half that clears the residue.",
    "transient_unit_patterns": [
      "app-*.scope",
      "snap.*.scope",
      "session-*.scope",
      "vte-spawn-*.scope"
    ],
    "_comment_memory": "Memory pressure, and why none of these is a plain threshold. On 2026-08-05 the 20:09 hourly run PASSED while mandark thrashed: RAM 5.3Gi of 7.5Gi (survivable-looking, which is exactly the problem), swap 3.8Gi of 4.0Gi, two OOM kills in 24h, and plasma-discover holding 209MB of swap idle since July 27. estate-health.sh had no memory or swap check at all. Swap is the leading indicator most tooling ignores; RAM is reported here as context with NO threshold, deliberately, because giving it one rebuilds the blind spot. swap_bands are percentages of SwapTotal, and what is reported is CROSSING one -- entering a band is news, sitting in it is not, because a 'swap > 75%' rule would have been true on this machine for weeks and been tuned out (the same class of failure the routing/flap-guard above exists to fix). swap_sustained_hours is the anti-silence guard on that: a host that sits in a band this long is news exactly ONCE per residency, so pure crossing detection cannot go quiet forever on a machine that climbed and stayed. 0 disables it.",
    "swap_bands": [
      50,
      75,
      90
    ],
    "swap_sustained_hours": 24,
    "_comment_swap_bands_by_host": "Per-device swap band overrides, keyed by the device's name in estate.devices; absent means the estate-wide swap_bands apply. This is the knob for potato, which has ~905MB of RAM (crt/POTATO.md) and is legitimately near the line all the time -- raising its bands here keeps it from reading as a permanent finding without special-casing it in a script. A device name containing a dot cannot be addressed as a dotted config key and silently falls back to the global list; don't name a device with a dot.",
    "swap_bands_by_host": {},
    "_comment_oom": "How far back the check looks for OOM kills in the kernel journal. An OOM kill is a fact that already happened, not a level to sample, so it is reported for every run inside this window and then ages out on its own -- no state, nothing to forget. If the kernel journal is not readable (user not in systemd-journal or adm) that is exit 2 / could-not-check, never a pass: journalctl exits 0 and prints nothing in that case, which is indistinguishable from a machine that has never OOMed.",
    "oom_lookback_hours": 24,
    "_comment_swap_squatters": "Processes sitting in swap while doing nothing -- memory a gauge cannot tell apart from memory in use, because only DURATION separates plasma-discover-idle-since-July-27 from something busy that happens to be paged out. A process is a squatter when it holds at least swap_squatter_min_mb of swap AND has been alive at least swap_squatter_min_idle_days AND has burned less than swap_squatter_max_cpu_pct of its lifetime on CPU. Lifetime-average CPU rather than an instantaneous sample is the point: nine days up and two seconds of CPU is dead weight whatever a one-second sample says. Reported as ONE aggregate finding, not one per process, so the alert layer pages once instead of every time the set shifts by one idle daemon. Local-only: it walks every /proc/<pid>/status, which is not something to run hourly against potato.",
    "swap_squatter_min_mb": 50,
    "swap_squatter_min_idle_days": 7,
    "swap_squatter_max_cpu_pct": 1,
    "_comment_memory_check_remote": "Whether reach=ssh linux devices also get the swap-band and OOM checks (the squatter scan is always local-only). Set false to keep the memory section to this host. Per-host swap bands above are the finer instrument; this is the off switch.",
    "memory_check_remote": true,
    "kdeconnect_device": "",
    "kdeconnect_device_name": "",
    "_comment_bluetooth": "Bluetooth mouse link stability -- remedies/bt-mouse-link-stability.sh. bt_adapter_usb_vid/pid identify the controller whose USB runtime PM must stay off; a suspended controller does no background scanning and a BLE peripheral can then never reconnect on its own. bt_reattach_warn_per_day is the honest witness: HID re-attach events in the journal are a record of a HUMAN re-pairing the mouse, not of the system recovering, so more than a handful a day means the link is still dropping no matter what the config says. wifi_conn/wifi_band pin Wi-Fi off 2.4 GHz, which shares the same radio and antennas as the mouse.",
    "bt_mouse_mac": "",
    "bt_adapter_usb_vid": "8087",
    "bt_adapter_usb_pid": "0029",
    "bt_reattach_warn_per_day": 4,
    "wifi_conn": "",
    "wifi_band": "a"
  },
  "_comment_follow_symlinked_dirs": "Whether the scan walks through a symlink that points at a directory. Default true, which is what a stow-style ~/dotfiles layout needs. Either way the symlink itself is now recorded in the snapshot with its target, so leaving the watched tree is visible rather than silent -- set this to false if a link starts pulling something large or private into journal/, which is committed to git.",
  "follow_symlinked_dirs": true,
  "watch": [
    "~/.local/bin",
    "~/.bashrc",
    "~/.profile",
    "~/.vimrc",
    "~/.gitconfig",
    "~/.tmux.conf",
    "~/.local/share/konsole",
    "~/.config/kglobalshortcutsrc",
    "~/.config/kwinrc",
    "~/.config/kdeglobals",
    "~/.config/plasma-org.kde.plasma.desktop-appletsrc",
    "~/.config/plasmashellrc",
    "~/.config/i3",
    "~/.config/sway",
    "~/.config/systemd/user",
    "~/.config/Synergy/Synergy.conf",
    "~/.local/share/Synergy/Synergy/LastConfig.cfg"
  ],
  "remote_hosts": [
    {
      "name": "dexter",
      "ssh_host": "dexter",
      "watch": [
        "C:\\Users\\Zach\\.gitconfig",
        "C:\\Users\\Zach\\dexter-scanner-forward.ps1",
        "C:\\Users\\Zach\\dexter-scanner-debug-wrapper.ps1",
        "C:\\Users\\Zach\\dexter-audio-server.py",
        "C:\\Users\\Zach\\dexter-whisper-server.py",
        "C:\\Users\\Zach\\set-default-audio.ps1",
        "C:\\Users\\Zach\\crtaudio.ps1",
        "C:\\Users\\Zach\\crt-whisper-server.bat",
        "C:\\Users\\Zach\\test-com.ps1",
        "C:\\Users\\Zach\\test-com2.ps1",
        "C:\\Users\\Zach\\update_task.ps1",
        "C:\\Users\\Zach\\nettest.py",
        "C:\\Users\\Zach\\nettest2.py",
        "C:\\Users\\Zach\\listdev.py",
        "C:\\Users\\Zach\\b64decode.py",
        "C:\\Users\\Zach\\b64decode2.py",
        "C:\\Users\\Zach\\AppData\\Roaming\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Start Menu\\Programs\\Startup"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "_comment_declared_footprint": "Paths that other projects have declared as intentional installs onto this or other hosts. Used by senechal's footprint_reconciliation feature to identify undeclared (orphaned) vs. missing-then-removed entries. Each project maintains its own cross-write declarations in its FOCUS.md ('declare your host footprint' rule per realisateur/BUILD-DISCIPLINE.md); this list hand-maintains those declarations for reconciliation. Directories here cover every file under them (tailing slashes optional). Paths are ~-expanded before comparison against scan results. Example: '~/.local/bin' covers every file senechal finds in ~/.local/bin. Can be empty if no footprint reconciliation is yet active.",
  "declared_footprint": [],
  "_comment_windows": "Named browser/app profiles and where their windows belong -- read by tools/browse, which launches through tools/spawn-here so the window lands on the declared desktop instead of wherever you happened to be looking when it finally drew. cmd is argv (list of strings). class is matched case-insensitively against WM_CLASS (use the lowercase browser name: it matches chromium's 'chromium','Chromium' and firefox's 'Navigator','firefox_firefox' alike). desktop is 1-based; omit it to mean 'whatever desktop you launched from'. geometry is 'X,Y,W,H' or 'maximize'; omit to leave the app's own remembered placement alone. Deliberately browser-agnostic: a chromium --profile-directory and a firefox -P are the same shape here.",
  "windows": {
    "profiles": {
      "personal": {
        "cmd": [
          "chromium",
          "--profile-directory=Default"
        ],
        "class": "chromium",
        "desktop": 1,
        "geometry": "0,0,960,1040"
      },
      "work": {
        "cmd": [
          "firefox",
          "-P",
          "work",
          "--new-window"
        ],
        "class": "firefox",
        "desktop": 2,
        "geometry": "maximize"
      }
    }
  }
}
